// A fused variant pattern tests only the TAG, so two arms carrying the
// SAME tag at different arities are indistinguishable to the kernel: the
// first one wins and its payload slots read as 0.
//
//   interp: 7    (`A(i64:7)` matches the arity-1 arm, n = 7)
//   jit:    -1   (the arity-0 arm's tag test passes first)
//
// Fusion verified separately: the CLI form
//     let x: [`A, `A(i64)] = `A(i64:7);
//     let r =
//     #[native]
//     select x { `A => i64:-1, `A(n) => n };
//     println("[r]")
// compiles in JIT mode (so `#[native]` passes — a genuinely fused
// region) and prints 7 / -1. The wrapper form below is the oracle pin.
//
// The union `[`A, `A(i64)]` is legal and typechecks — a variant's
// identity is (tag, arity), and the typechecker keeps the two cases
// separate (it merges same-tag/same-ARITY cases payload-wise, so
// `[`A(i64), `A(f64)]` collapses to `` `A([i64,f64]) `` but these do
// not). Both arms are live: dropping either is a compile error (03).
//
// ROOT CAUSE, exact. The node-walk's `StructurePattern::is_match`
// (node/pattern.rs ~574-588) discriminates on the runtime
// REPRESENTATION and the payload ARITY: a 0-bind pattern requires
// `Value::String(s) && s == tag`; an n-bind pattern requires
// `Value::Array(a) && a.len() == binds.len() + 1 && a[0] == tag`. The
// fused lowering (fusion/emit/select.rs ~1200) reduces the whole test
// to one call to `graphix_variant_tag_eq`, which
// (fusion/emit_helpers.rs ~1011-1025) accepts EITHER representation and
// compares only the tag — no length check. The emitter's only
// structural guard is `elts.len() == pbinds.len()` against the arm's
// OWN frozen predicate, which says nothing about the runtime value. So
// both arms compile to the SAME condition (a CLIF dump shows two
// `call fn123(v12, v13, <same interned tag ptr>)` sites) and the first
// one textually wins.
//
// Other faces: 01 (arity 1 vs 2), 02 (the reactive escalation — the
// kernel goes SILENT), 03 (no annotation needed; survives a cross-kernel
// call).
//
// Fix: pass the expected payload count into `graphix_variant_tag_eq`
// (one extra argument at its single call site, fusion/emit/select.rs
// ~1200) and have the helper enforce representation AND arity — `n == 0`
// requires `Value::String(s) && s == tag`; `n > 0` requires
// `Value::Array(a) && a.len() == n + 1 && a[0] == tag`. It costs nothing
// when tags are distinct, and it also hardens the `variant_payload_read`s
// downstream, which today rely on the tag test for their index being in
// range. A sound minimal alternative: DE-FUSE the select when
// `kernel_abi::variant_cases(scrut_typ)` contains a duplicated tag — the
// tag alone is a complete discriminator exactly when all tags are
// distinct, which is the common case.
//
// A wrongly-taken arm binds slots the value doesn't have;
// `variant_payload_read` is totalized (crash-triage-jul2026) so those
// read as 0 — a wrong value, deterministic, not a pointer leak (three
// runs, identical).
//
// Related: select-lit-leaf-union-slot-aug2026 is the same shape of bug
// one level down (a literal leaf over a union-typed slot, fixed by
// requiring the leaf's static type to freeze to the literal's prim
// family). This is the variant-arity twin and wants the same treatment:
// when the kernel cannot distinguish two arms, DE-FUSE rather than
// guess.
{ let x: [`A, `A(i64)] = `A(i64:7); select x { `A => i64:-1, `A(n) => n } }
